WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:03.220
Picture a unit that is measured on how many applications

2
00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:07.300
it processes each month. One colleague's numbers are noticeably lower

3
00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:10.560
than everyone else's. When her supervisor looks closer, she learns

4
00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:14.380
why. This colleague spends extra time arranging interpreters and accessible

5
00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:17.080
formats, so people can complete the process fully.

6
00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:20.320
So the measure says she is behind, and the reality

7
00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:22.660
says she may be doing some of the most important

8
00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:25.360
work on the team. That tension seems to sit right

9
00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:26.540
at the center of today's style.

10
00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:31.890
It does. Transactional leadership is built on clear exchange. The

11
00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:35.510
leader sets clear goals and measurable standards, recognizes people when

12
00:00:35.510 --> 00:00:37.770
they meet them, and steps in promptly when work falls

13
00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:40.470
short. It does not try to reshape the culture or

14
00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:44.110
inspire deep change. It makes the existing system run reliably,

15
00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:45.810
predictably and fairly.

16
00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:48.680
That sounds a little dry when you say it out

17
00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:51.760
loud. But I suspect many people actually appreciate knowing exactly

18
00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:53.380
where they stand with their supervisor.

19
00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:57.690
Many do. Researchers describe two parts to it. One is

20
00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:00.770
contingent reward: being clear about what is expected and reliably

21
00:01:00.770 --> 00:01:03.750
recognizing people when they deliver it. The other is management

22
00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:07.390
by exception: watching performance against standards and stepping in when

23
00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:10.670
something slips. When the measures are fair and visible, people

24
00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:12.530
know exactly what success looks like.

25
00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:16.000
Give me a picture of a transactional leader on a

26
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:18.740
typical week. What would I see in their meetings and

27
00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:19.200
in their feedback?

28
00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:24.090
Clear targets for each role, such as turnaround times, accuracy

29
00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:27.970
rates or project milestones. Prompt recognition for people who meet

30
00:01:27.970 --> 00:01:31.630
them. Quick, specific feedback when something misses the standard, based

31
00:01:31.630 --> 00:01:35.330
on the written expectation rather than personal criticism. Meetings that

32
00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:38.770
focus on deliverables and deadlines. And a preference for proven

33
00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:40.710
procedures over untested experiments.

34
00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:45.360
Where does this approach genuinely shine? I imagine there are

35
00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:47.580
kinds of work where it is not just useful but

36
00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:47.900
necessary.

37
00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:54.390
High volume, regulated, deadline driven work: processing benefits, payroll, technical

38
00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:58.270
help desks, grant compliance. People need clear standards and consistent

39
00:01:58.270 --> 00:02:01.970
accountability there. And the page makes a striking point: even

40
00:02:01.970 --> 00:02:05.410
the most inspiring strategy will collapse without this discipline underneath

41
00:02:05.410 --> 00:02:09.610
it, meaning clear milestones, named owners and consistent follow-through.

42
00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:13.020
And yet there is a ceiling, isn't there? What can

43
00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:15.300
this style not do, no matter how well it is

44
00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:15.520
run?

45
00:02:16.190 --> 00:02:20.070
The page puts it simply: it buys compliance, not commitment.

46
00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:23.690
People rarely go above and beyond, challenge broken systems or

47
00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:26.470
help across teams if those things are not rewarded. And

48
00:02:26.470 --> 00:02:29.250
in public service, where pay scales and budgets limit rewards,

49
00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:32.070
a leader who relies only on incentives runs out of

50
00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:32.510
levers quickly.

51
00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:36.260
There is a famous idea about measures that seems to

52
00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:39.120
apply here. Can you explain it in plain terms for

53
00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:40.380
listeners who have not heard of it?

54
00:02:40.850 --> 00:02:44.510
It is often called Goodhart's law: when a measure becomes

55
00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:46.930
a target, it stops being a good measure. If a

56
00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:49.990
unit is judged only on how fast cases close, people

57
00:02:49.990 --> 00:02:53.190
may rush complex situations or avoid hard cases to protect

58
00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:56.410
their numbers. The dashboard turns green while the outcomes for

59
00:02:56.410 --> 00:02:57.370
real people get worse.

60
00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:02.200
Which brings us back to the colleague arranging interpreters. How

61
00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:06.060
does the page describe transactional leadership's effect on fair service

62
00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:06.740
and on staff?

63
00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:11.870
As double-edged, and surprisingly important. On the helpful side, it

64
00:03:11.870 --> 00:03:15.310
can turn equity from good intentions into real accountability, by

65
00:03:15.310 --> 00:03:19.110
building things like timely accommodations or accessible documents into how

66
00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:23.070
performance is measured. And clear, written criteria protect people from

67
00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:25.750
vague judgments, such as someone just not feeling like leadership

68
00:03:25.750 --> 00:03:28.450
material, which is exactly where bias tends to hide.

69
00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:31.680
And on the harmful side, I suspect the opening story

70
00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:34.740
is the clearest example we could ask for. Measures that

71
00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:37.140
quietly punish the people doing the most careful work.

72
00:03:37.690 --> 00:03:41.330
Exactly. If a worker is measured only on calls closed

73
00:03:41.330 --> 00:03:44.350
per hour, spending extra time with someone who uses an

74
00:03:44.350 --> 00:03:47.610
interpreter or has a cognitive disability makes her numbers look

75
00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:51.650
worse. Poorly designed measures can reward unequal service. They can

76
00:03:51.650 --> 00:03:55.210
also miss invisible work entirely, such as mentoring newer colleagues

77
00:03:55.210 --> 00:03:57.650
or working around tools that are not accessible.

78
00:03:58.540 --> 00:04:02.540
So what should that supervisor actually do? Telling her to

79
00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:05.800
speed up feels wrong, but simply excusing her from the

80
00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:07.640
target does not feel quite right either.

81
00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:12.590
Recognize the access work openly, and propose adding it to

82
00:04:12.590 --> 00:04:16.710
how the unit measures quality, alongside the count. Clear expectations

83
00:04:16.710 --> 00:04:19.850
stay, but now they reward the work that makes the

84
00:04:19.850 --> 00:04:24.190
process fair. Access becomes the standard for everyone, rather than

85
00:04:24.190 --> 00:04:25.450
one person's extra effort.

86
00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:29.280
That feels like the real lesson of the style: keep

87
00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:32.160
the clarity, but measure the right things. What else does

88
00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:34.400
the page recommend for leaders who rely on measures?

89
00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:38.770
Look at every measure and ask whether hitting it encourages

90
00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:42.770
people to rush past someone with language, disability or complex

91
00:04:42.770 --> 00:04:46.930
needs, and balance speed with quality. Include fair people management

92
00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:50.830
and accessibility in how supervisors themselves are evaluated. And pair

93
00:04:50.830 --> 00:04:53.910
the measures with purpose, so the team understands why the

94
00:04:53.910 --> 00:04:56.710
standards matter to people's dignity and to public trust.

95
00:04:57.540 --> 00:05:00.500
Here is a question to carry with you. What important

96
00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:02.560
work on your team does not show up in any

97
00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:05.360
measure, and who is doing it? Thank you for listening.
